<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">São Paulo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">São Paulo is a region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, now a state, centred on the city of the same name. In the nineteenth century it became the heart of Brazil's booming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> economy. The São Paulo landowning elite increasingly supported the Republican Party, contributing to the fall of the monarchy in 1889.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sao-paulo.jpg" alt="São Paulo" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agent010 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marginal_Pinheiros_e_Jockey_Club.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…history's more unusual acts of dynastic improvisation, when the Portuguese heir and Prince Regent Pedro, on the banks of the Ipiranga River, a stream near <b>São Paulo</b>, on 7 September 1822, proclaimed Brazilian independence with the Grito do Ipiranga and was acclaimed Emperor Pedro I on 12 October.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Supported by the king, Nóbrega, Anchieta and their companions founded missions at Salvador and <b>São Paulo</b> and gathered Indigenous people into aldeias where they worked and were evangelised, a policy that after 1557 accepted force; epidemics, forced removals and colonists' raids for labourers made the aldeias places people…</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Brazil, whose Law of the Free Womb of 1871 had declared free the children born to enslaved mothers, came last: as abolitionist clubs spread and the enslaved fled the plantations of <b>São Paulo</b> in their thousands, Princess Isabel signed the Golden Law on 13 May 1888.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1990 the Workers’ Party of Brazil launched the <b>São Paulo</b> Forum, whose members were associated with the pink tide of left-wing governments in Latin America; in Venezuela Hugo Chávez proclaimed “Socialism of the 21st century”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>…the remodelled Hagia Irene, and in modern times the Kronstadt Naval Cathedral reproduces it almost exactly in size and geometry, while churches from Sofia and London to St. Louis and <b>São Paulo</b>, and synagogues such as the Hurva Synagogue and the Great Synagogue of Florence, drew on it.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It opens with the proclamation of independence on the banks of the Ipiranga, the stream near <b>São Paulo</b> where the future Pedro I declared Brazil free of Portugal, and goes on to celebrate the homeland, borrowing phrases from Gonçalves Dias's poem &quot;Canção do exílio&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/brazilian-national-anthem">Brazilian National Anthem</a></li></ul></section>
